                    coach_r2_systems:       `<strong>Now the system can steer.</strong> A focused scorecard, the gameable KPI refused, a faster close and overhead allocated by cost-to-serve drivers — managers can finally see a customer turn before the quarter is gone. You changed the information, not just the prices.`,
                    coach_r2_nostress:      `<strong>You launched a volume metric without stress-testing it for gaming.</strong> Every metric invites a perverse response; the time to anticipate it is before launch, not after order count spikes. Walk each KPI through “how would a clever rep beat this without helping margin?”`,
                    coach_r3_segment:       `<strong>That is portfolio surgery with CLV, not a chainsaw.</strong> Reprice or redesign service for the structural losers, develop the growable mid-tail, and exit only the genuinely unfixable. Margin rises, the leak closes, and you keep the lifetime value a blunt cut would have handed a competitor.`,
                    coach_r3_payonly:       `<strong>Flipping commissions to pure profitability overnight is right in aim, reckless in method.</strong> Cost-to-serve was invisible to reps yesterday; today their pay depends on it with no ramp. Three of your top five reps are updating their CVs. Changing the incentive was right — doing it without a transition was not.`,
                    coach_r3_payrevenue:    `<strong>Leaving pay on revenue keeps the perverse incentive intact.</strong> You can redesign every KPI you like, but variable comp drives behaviour toward whatever it rewards — and yours still rewards the accounts destroying margin. The measurement changed; the money did not.`,
                    coach_r3_overnight:     `<strong>Pure profitability pay with no transition: the textbook talent shock.</strong> Three top reps resigned within the month and took key accounts with them; revenue dropped and the margin gain evaporated. How do you change behaviour without losing the people who execute it? Phase it, guarantee it, retrain.`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>Cutting pay overnight for reps on loss-making accounts punishes them for a system you designed.</strong> They did exactly what revenue pay told them to. A unilateral cut reads as a betrayal — the best ones leave and take the relationships with them. Transition, do not ambush.`,
                    coach_r3_firetail:      `<strong>You exited the whole bottom 20% and the margin jumped — but a third were growable mid-tail customers you handed to a competitor.</strong> Their volume covered fixed cost, so your unit cost on what remains rose, and the lifetime value walked out the door. Which of those had high CLV — and how would you have known?`,
                    coach_r3_ignoretail:    `<strong>Leaving the tail untouched keeps the €6.5M leaking.</strong> “The volume covers fixed cost” is the comfort that protects loss-makers forever. The discipline is to act <em>differently</em> on each account — reprice, redesign, develop or exit — not to freeze because acting feels risky.`,
