                    coach_r3_dump:          `<strong>You let the customer walk without checking their lifetime value.</strong> Some of the tail are early-stage or strategic accounts that would be valuable if developed. Exiting on this quarter’s profit alone can destroy future value — segment by CLV before you let anyone go.`,
                    coach_r3_nobudget:      `<strong>No change budget is a bet that behaviour changes for free.</strong> Re-basing pay needs a transition guarantee, reps need retraining, and repricing needs the pricing system to actually issue the surcharges. Starve the change and the redesign stays on the slide, not in the field.`,
                    coach_r4_structural:    `<strong>That is the story a board can trust.</strong> Margin up, reps demonstrably selling toward profitable accounts, CLV protected and the close fast enough to steer. You are not claiming a number — you are showing a system that changed behaviour and will keep doing so.`,
                    coach_r4_pricepop:      `<strong>Leading with the headline pop invites the obvious probe.</strong> “Is this the system working, or one-off price hikes that reverse when customers tender your contracts?” If you cannot show changed behaviour underneath the number, the board reads the pop as a sugar high — and they are usually right.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>“Nothing really had to change” surrenders the whole mandate.</strong> The board hired you because the revenue-only system was destroying margin. Defending it now says the diagnosis was wrong and the €6.5M leak was imaginary. It was not.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is the evidence that proves durability.</strong> A goal-congruence score showing reps shifted their mix toward profitable accounts — not just a quarter of surcharges — is the difference between structural and cosmetic. The board can see the behaviour changed, not only the number.`,
                    coach_r4_assert:        `<strong>“Trust me, the team has changed” is not evidence.</strong> A PE board runs on causality. Without the goal-congruence data and the CLV of retained accounts, they will assume the margin is a one-off and discount it. Show the behaviour, not your conviction.`,
                    coach_r4_deflect:       `<strong>Deflecting the durability question confirms their fear.</strong> Avoiding causality tells the board the margin is a price-hike sugar high you cannot defend. The whole point of the control system was to make the improvement legible and durable — show it, do not dodge it.`,
                    coach_r4_addvolume:     `<strong>Adding a volume KPI back undoes the redesign.</strong> The revenue chase is exactly the behaviour you spent a quarter unwinding; reinstating it re-opens the leak and re-games the system. Control design is never “done,” but the answer is to refine the guard, not to restore the perverse incentive.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>“The system is finished” misreads control as a project, not a discipline.</strong> Every metric drifts and every incentive gets gamed eventually; a controller keeps refining the weakest KPI and tightening its guard. Declaring it done is how the sprawl creeps back.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Loaded true cost-to-serve, named the few drivers, called the perverse incentive.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Ranked on the wrong number or left cost-to-serve unloaded.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Focused scorecard anchored on customer margin; refused the gameable KPI.`,
                    sum_r2_gamed:           `Launched a volume KPI that rewards the leak — Goodhart’s law.`,
                    sum_r2_sprawl:          `Kept the 40+ metrics — a dashboard that focuses no one.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Scorecard or information system under-built.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Phased pay re-base, CLV-segmented portfolio, held the repricing line.`,
                    sum_r3_fired:           `Fired the whole tail — margin pop, lifetime value gone.`,
                    sum_r3_flight:          `Re-based pay without a transition — top reps walked.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Pay or portfolio only partially aligned.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Structural story, goal-congruence evidence, kept refining the system.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Claimed a pop, deflected causality, or re-opened the perverse incentive.`,

                    insight_structural:     `Control system working: you loaded the true cost-to-serve, focused the scorecard, re-based pay with a transition, and acted on the portfolio by CLV — so operating margin reached the 9% target on changed behaviour, not a one-off pop. Reps are selling toward profitable accounts, the leak is closed and the lifetime value of the book is intact. This is structural performance improvement, and the goal-congruence evidence proves it.`,
                    insight_firetail:       `Value-destroying cut: firing the entire loss-making tail produced a fast margin jump, but a third of those accounts were growable mid-tail customers you handed to a competitor — along with the fixed-cost coverage they provided, which pushed unit cost up on the volume that remains. CLV walked out the door. Profitability cuts that ignore lifetime value destroy future value.`,
                    insight_repflight:      `Rep flight: re-basing pay without a transition was the right aim executed the wrong way. Top reps resigned and took key accounts with them; revenue dropped and the margin gain evaporated. Changing the incentive was correct — doing it without a guarantee, retraining and stay-conversations was not. You cannot change behaviour by losing the people who execute it.`,
                    insight_gamed:          `Gamed metric: a volume KPI in the system rewards the very small-frequent-order behaviour bleeding your margin. Order count is up, average order size is down, and cost-to-serve is worse — you measured motion, not margin. A measure that becomes a target stops being a good measure; the leak never fully closed.`,
                    insight_cosmetic:       `Cosmetic, not structural: margin moved on one-off price increases while the goal-congruence score barely did — and customers have begun putting your contracts out to tender. A sugar high reverses next quarter. Without evidence that reps actually sell differently, the board is right to discount the pop.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed result: parts of the redesign landed, but the diagnosis, scorecard, pay and portfolio did not fully reinforce each other — enough seams that the margin improvement is not yet clearly structural, and the board can still question whether it is durable.`,
                    insight_sprawl:         `You kept too many KPIs: a dashboard with 40+ metrics focuses no one, so managers could not see which handful actually move the 9% target. A control system that does not focus attention does not control anything — the line of sight to margin was never built.`,
                    insight_infounfixed:    `You changed the prices but not the information system: an 11-day close and revenue-based overhead allocation mean you still see customers turn unprofitable a quarter too late, and high-touch accounts still look artificially cheap. Timely, well-allocated information is the precondition for steering — without it, control is always retrospective.`,
                    insight_clv_low:        `The lifetime value of your book fell: aggressive cuts or a caved repricing line cost you customer relationships worth more than this quarter’s margin. CLV is the discipline that separates the loss-makers to exit from the ones to develop — and it slipped.`,
                    insight_leak_open:      `The margin leak never fully closed: the loss-making tail is still bleeding because cost-to-serve was under-loaded, a metric stayed gameable, or the portfolio was left untouched. The €6.5M was the prize — and some of it is still leaking.`,
                    insight_margin_no_behaviour: `Margin hit the target but goal-congruence did not follow: the number moved without the behaviour, which is the signature of a cosmetic gain. The board will ask whether it lasts — and the honest answer is that a margin without changed behaviour usually does not.`,

                    hint_repflight:         `Top reps have walked — the margin gain is eroding and the relationships went with them. Re-basing pay needs a transition guarantee, retraining and stay-conversations, not a shock.`,
                    hint_firetail:          `You fired the whole tail — margin popped but you handed growable mid-tail accounts and their lifetime value to a competitor, and fixed-cost absorption capped the gain. Segment by CLV before exiting anyone.`,
                    hint_gamed:             `A gameable volume KPI is in the system, rewarding the small-frequent-order behaviour that bleeds margin. The leak will not close until you refuse it and anchor on customer margin.`,
                    hint_infounfixed:       `You are repricing on a broken information system — an 11-day close and revenue-based allocation mean you steer a quarter too late. Speed up the close and allocate overhead by cost-to-serve.`,
                    hint_congruence:        `Goal-congruence is low: incentives and metrics still pull behaviour away from margin. Re-base pay toward customer profitability and anchor the scorecard on customer margin.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — margin at target, behaviour aligned and CLV protected. Land it: lead the board with the structural story and the goal-congruence evidence, and keep refining the weakest KPI.`,

